"""Prompts for the inputs: the profile census and profile-context compatibility scoring."""

PROFILE_RULES = [{'id': 'P1', 'gate': 'hard', 'text': 'All required profile fields are present, interpretable, and expressed at the specified attribute granularity; no field contains malformed, ambiguous, or out-of-ontology content.'}, {'id': 'P2', 'gate': 'hard', 'text': 'The profile contains no direct contradiction or impossible combination across its stated attributes.'}, {'id': 'P3', 'gate': 'hard', 'text': 'The age, education, occupation, and relationship attributes admit a plausible life course without requiring an impossible or highly implausible chronology.'}, {'id': 'P4', 'gate': 'soft', 'text': 'The profile attributes are mutually compatible as an ordinary individual. Uncommon combinations are permitted and no conventional demographic, educational, occupational, health, or socioeconomic association is required.'}, {'id': 'P5', 'gate': 'soft', 'text': 'The profile does not read as a caricature, stereotype bundle, or generator artifact in which many attributes are mechanically chosen to reinforce the same demographic archetype.'}]

CENSUS_SYSTEM = 'You are a data-quality checker for synthetic fictional profiles. Judge the\nprofile alone as a coherent fictional person. Whether this person fits any\ndownstream communication context is out of scope and judged elsewhere.\n\nRules P1-P3 are hard gates. Rules P4-P5 are soft diagnostics.\n\nP1 (hard): All required profile fields are present, interpretable, and expressed at the specified attribute granularity; no field contains malformed, ambiguous, or out-of-ontology content.\nP2 (hard): The profile contains no direct contradiction or impossible combination across its stated attributes.\nP3 (hard): The age, education, occupation, and relationship attributes admit a plausible life course without requiring an impossible or highly implausible chronology.\nP4 (soft): The profile attributes are mutually compatible as an ordinary individual. Uncommon combinations are permitted and no conventional demographic, educational, occupational, health, or socioeconomic association is required.\nP5 (soft): The profile does not read as a caricature, stereotype bundle, or generator artifact in which many attributes are mechanically chosen to reinforce the same demographic archetype.\n\nCalibration. P2 covers direct contradiction or impossibility only. P3 asks\nwhether one person could plausibly have developed this way over time. P4 is\ncompatibility, not necessity: no occupation requires matching education,\nuncommon combinations are permitted, and you must not require conventional\ndemographic, educational, occupational, health, or socioeconomic\nassociations. Never enforce population statistics. Fail a profile only for\nimpossible, contradictory, malformed, or generator-artifact content.\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else, of the form\n{"P1": "PASS", "P2": "PASS", "P3": "PASS", "P4": "PASS", "P5": "PASS",\n "reasons": {}} where every rule is "PASS" or "FAIL" and reasons carries\none short sentence for each FAIL (empty object when everything passes).'

RUBRIC_RULES = [{'id': 'R1', 'short': 'Eligibility', 'text': 'For open recommendation/chat and ordinary consumer-support contexts, is ordinary adult/user/customer eligibility enough; otherwise does the profile provide the needed appointment, application, employment, licence, residency, housing, healthcare-patient, or regulated-status cue?'}, {'id': 'R2', 'short': 'Sender standing', 'text': 'Does the profile make this person, not merely any adult, a natural category-level sender, allowing open advice/recommendation and ordinary support channels but requiring profile support for application, work, housing, finance onboarding, government, course, professional, or regulated channels?'}, {'id': 'R3', 'short': 'Motivation', 'text': 'Does the profile give a plausible reason to engage this context, counting ordinary or routine needs it already implies (a specific trigger is a bonus, not a requirement) and failing only when nothing beyond generic adult possibility connects the person, without inventing events such as a move, job search, or honeymoon?'}, {'id': 'R4', 'short': 'Typicality', 'text': 'Is this a common everyday interaction for someone with this profile, not a rare or contrived edge case?'}, {'id': 'R5', 'short': 'Evidence', 'text': 'Is the fit supported by at least two directly relevant profile details, rather than a single weak age, health, or income signal?'}, {'id': 'R6', 'short': 'Consistency', 'text': 'Do occupation/education positively align with the implied role, or are they irrelevant for a simple personal/medical/recommendation context; for specialized administrative contexts, is there more than mere no-conflict support?'}, {'id': 'R7', 'short': 'Resources', 'text': 'Does the profile include a burden-specific cue for the time, money, travel, paperwork, scheduling, or digital-access load, or is the context a low-burden open recommendation/chat?'}, {'id': 'R8', 'short': 'Access', 'text': 'Is the recipient or service practically accessible through patient/customer/tenant/employee/student/licence-holder status, a local public agency, ordinary customer support, or an open public channel, with explicit support for local appointment, regulated, government, housing, and professional services?'}, {'id': 'R9', 'short': 'Authorship', 'text': 'Is self-authored message/email/chat a normal or clearly common channel with individualized explanation beyond IDs, checkboxes, or search terms, rather than a pure search/recommendation/reservation widget, template-only, proxy, institutional, automated, or routine self-service flow?'}, {'id': 'R10', 'short': 'Form', 'text': 'Is the register and level of detail plausible for how this profile would write to this recipient, judging tone, formality, and specificity rather than raw length?'}]

COMPATIBILITY_SYSTEM = 'You are a careful rater of how naturally a specific person would author a given message, judging only plausibility/fit (not privacy or quality) via a fixed ten-criterion binary naturalness rubric (each criterion met=1 / not-met=0; score = number of criteria met, integer 0-10; no hidden gate override). Output strict parseable JSON only: no markdown fences, no prose outside the JSON, no trailing commas.'

COMPATIBILITY_INTRO = 'For each candidate context, judge independently how NATURAL it is for this person to write in it by evaluating TEN binary naturalness criteria (met=1 / not-met=0), using ONLY the profile and context text (do not invent facts). Use the whole 0-10 range. Generic adult plausibility is not enough for a high score; award a criterion point only when the profile or context explicitly supports it. If support is uncertain or generic, mark that criterion 0:'

COMPATIBILITY_RULES_TAIL = 'score = number of criteria met, integer 0-10. Do not apply any hidden gate override; if R1 or R2 is 0, the score is still the sum of R1..R10.\nCalibration: 5 means plausible but generic; 7-8 means good fit with a few weak points; 9 means very strong with one concrete weakness; 10 is rare and requires explicit or near-required support across motivation, sender standing, resources, access, authorship mode, and form.\nDo not invent hidden life events from stable labels: relationship status alone does not imply moving, honeymoon travel, joint accounts, utility transfers, job search, or a current family event; occupation alone does not imply a current interview/course/project; health alone supports medical contexts only when the context naturally asks for symptom/care explanation.\nProfessional licence nuance: for licence-renewal contexts, an occupation that commonly requires licences, permits, board registration, food-safety credentials, environmental/professional certifications, or continuing credentials can support R1/R2/R6/R8 even without an explicit licence field; still mark R3=0 unless the profile/context gives renewal timing or a current credential issue.\nDoctor-appointment nuance: a health attribute supports medical-intake relevance, but do not give 9-10 merely because any health condition is present. Reserve 9-10 for serious, chronic, function-limiting, or appointment-specific conditions with enough detail for substantive triage; mild/common or loosely related conditions should usually land below 9.\nFor R10, judge only register plausibility -- whether this person could write an appropriate, individualized message to this recipient. Do not penalize verbosity or an assumed target length; most everyday requests pass. Mark R10=0 only for a genuine register mismatch: a highly technical, legal, or officious message from a profile with no relevant background, or a context needing far more personal self-disclosure than this person would volunteer.\nHigh-score cap: if R3 is 0, the score should normally be at most 8; if both R3 and R10 are 0, normally at most 7. Scores of 9-10 require a current/specific motivation or a near-required recurring need plus a naturally detailed authored message.\nGeneric routine consumer tasks should land around 4-6 when standing/access are ordinary but motivation, profile detail, authorship depth, or length are weak; they should not collapse to 0 unless eligibility/standing truly contradict the profile.\nFor R1/R2/R7/R8, do not use the context name or recipient alone as evidence; require a profile cue or an explicitly open/low-burden channel.\nReply as JSON mapping each context_id to {"score": <int 0-10>, "rubric": {"R1":0/1, ..., "R10":0/1}, "rubric_rationales": {"R1":"2-6 words", ..., "R10":"2-6 words"}, "rationale": "<one compact sentence naming the main failed criteria>"}. Every context must include every R1..R10 key in both rubric objects. Keep output compact; use fragments and do not repeat full profile/context text.'
